Sure! To extract all key‑value pairs that come after a specific key (e.g., `"userId"`) in your `dataAnalysisContext`, you can use the `getNextKeyValues` function.  
Below is an example of how you would call it. If you already have the JSON representation of `dataAnalysisContext`, just replace the placeholder string with that JSON.

```json
{
  "ctx": "{\"userId\":12345,\"name\":\"Alice\",\"email\":\"alice@example.com\",\"age\":30,\"address\":{\"street\":\"123 Main St\",\"city\":\"Metropolis\"}}",
  "currentKey": "\"userId\""
}
```

**Function call**

```json
{
  "name": "getNextKeyValues",
  "arguments": {
    "ctx": "{\"userId\":12345,\"name\":\"Alice\",\"email\":\"alice@example.com\",\"age\":30,\"address\":{\"street\":\"123 Main St\",\"city\":\"Metropolis\"}}",
    "currentKey": "\"userId\""
  }
}
```

When you run this, the function will return all key‑value pairs that follow `"userId"` until it encounters a new nested object or array. If you need to extract pairs after a different key, just change the `currentKey` value accordingly.
